/*****************************************************************************
    jitSlotT -- OP ONE OF THE SLOT MIGRATION, the jitOp specimen. 2026-08-17.

    Certifies that the multiply operator emits through its gJitEmitter slot
    rather than through the `if jitting` gate inside opMultiply.

    ⚠⚠ THE LOAD-BEARING ROW IS THE SLOT COUNT, NOT THE PRODUCT, AND THAT IS NOT
    A STYLE PREFERENCE. The migration is value-transparent BY DESIGN: a migrated
    multiply and an unmigrated one emit the same IR and return the same number,
    because a presence-gated fork is supposed to be invisible to everything
    except the fork. The products below are necessary and NOT sufficient -- a
    rung asserting only them stays green with the mechanism deleted.

    H7 NEGATIVE CONTROL, measured 2026-08-17 rather than asserted. The
    jitEmitter registration was removed from the Operators entry for the star
    operator in incant setup -- read at run time, so no rebuild -- and jitJR run
    both ways:

        registered      slot count 1    results 6 and 24    degrade 0
        NOT registered  slot count 0    results 6 and 24    degrade 0

    Products IDENTICAL down the column. Only the count moves.

    WHY A COUNT AND NOT A TRACE LINE. H4. An absence check on a printed marker
    passes on the day somebody deletes the marker. The count is printed
    unconditionally by jitRunAction beside the degrade and compile counts and is
    compared against a value; the ladder additionally fails if the line is
    MISSING, so the discriminator cannot pass by vanishing.

    ⚠ FIRE TWO USES DIFFERENT OPERANDS ON PURPOSE. 42 and 45 differ, so a folded
    constant or a stale result cannot satisfy both -- the injectivity rule,
    copied from jitJR.

    ⚠ THE OUTPUT FIELD IS INITIALIZED BEFORE THE FIRE, AND IT HAS TO BE. An
    uninitialized field carries no data, so reading it back returns its own TAG
    -- the string jsOut -- which is bear-trap 26 and cost this fixture a vacuous
    row in its first draft. With the field initialized the compiled store lands
    and the read-back is a real value. Measured both ways.

    ⚠ THE JITTED HALF IS FIRST, bear-trap 25: testing routes by isCoded and an
    interpreted run consumes it, so an oracle placed above the jitted half
    silently measures the interpreter at exit 0. The oracle is a FIELD read in
    the jitJR position, which is the correct use of it.
*****************************************************************************/

register(JitLadder);
define
    jsA;
    jsB;
    jsOut;
    jsMul code={
        jsOut = jsA * jsB;
        };
    ;

jsA = 6; jsB = 7; jsOut = 0;
print "JS fire 1 setup : jsA =" jsA " jsB =" jsB:;
testing(jsMul);
print "JS fire 1 result: jsOut =" jsOut " (want 42)":;

jsA = 5; jsB = 9; jsOut = 0;
print "JS fire 2 setup : jsA =" jsA " jsB =" jsB " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JS fire 2 result: jsOut =" jsOut " (want 45)":;

jsA = 6; jsB = 7; jsOut = 0;
jsMul();
print "JS interpreted  : jsOut =" jsOut " (the oracle -- want 42)":;
print "JITSLOT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
